 refugees nor did it  rid Syria of the remaining chemical  weapons I know you all remember in 2009  when President Obama laid out a very  bold and aggressive agenda to seek peace  and security by reducing the number of  nuclear weapons eight years of  diplomatic efforts followed the  president's pledge and after nearly a  decade of persistence the Obama  administration together with our allies  were able to negotiate a deal that put a  lid on Iran's nuclear program and  created the most expensive and intrusive  nuclear verification regime ever  negotiated I was pleased to be part of  working with the White House Secretary  Moniz on a very difficult joint  comprehensive plan of action and it was  really difficult to get this through but  musing and I remembered we had a day  public awareness red phone we called to  Iran I authored the legislation to end  our nation's counterproductive  no-contact policy so we've been doing a  lot on Capitol Hill prior to the joint  plan of action to build the public  support for that this deal has and will  continue to make the world a safer place  during the Obama presidency we were also  reminded that the power of diplomacy is  not just reserved for the executive  branch members of Congress are also  uniquely positioned to move us closer to  a fair and more just and safe society  when they embrace the power of diplomacy  but now we must make sure that it does  not unravel we have to be very vigilant  on this Iran nuclear deal we've made  history together all of us over the past  eight years but I think we can all agree  that this is at risk with this  administration from the constant saber  rattling  between President Trump and King Kim  Jong  to tense rhetoric being released from  the Trump administration we've been  reaching and we I think have reached a  widest watershed moment in history I'm a  member of the military and Veterans  Affairs subcommittee I recently was part  of a congressional delegation to visit  Japan and South Korea and let me tell  you a couple of weeks ago I was in the  region it reinforced my concerns about  the potential consequences of rising  tensions and militarization we went to  the DMZ and I saw how volatile the  region is and how close we are to a  nuclear war we visited Tokyo and Okinawa  and the memory and impact of Hiroshima  and Nagasaki are very real and on  everyone's heart in mind  sadly our allies in the Korean Peninsula  have come to live with the constant  threat of conflict and that should not  be the case  now after returning from the region it  became clearer than ever to me that we  are not alone in our fear of what this  president's temperament may cause our  international allies also believe that  the president is capable of tweeting us  into a nuclear war that is why we must  continue to put pressure on this  president to give comprar give Congress  a comprehensive strategy for deterring  North Korea that puts diplomacy and non  military strategies at the forefront we  must also prevent any president  especially president Trump from  launching nuclear weapons without a  declaration from Congress we have to  have this declaration you can't use the  2001 AUMF we must also make sure  Congress immediately demands that the  United States adopt a no nuclear first  policy to place a check on the executive  branch no first strike its are proud to  have led my colleagues in a letter to  President Obama at the end of his  presidency urging him to adopt his  common-sense policy this common-sense  policy and so also I'm  to co-sponsor HR I think you know this  bill 669 which would prohibit a first  you strike from the president  absent a declaration of war by Congress  we must pass this bill and Congress must  stop being and missing in action if you  ask me we have a press conference  Wednesday 500,000 petitions we received  in support of this bill 500,000 people  are waking up to the threat of nuclear  annihilation and beginning to lobby  Congress and yes this is a political  struggle mobilizing on campuses  registering people to vote holding  members of Congress accountable such as  Erika laid out electing public officials  who support this agenda and taking out  those who don't and so I would urge you  to look at what's in your packet  mobilizing on campuses in response to  Trump's nuclear threat because it really  sets forth the political agenda that we  all need to adopt and need to work on  this day forward so many of you were  part of the effort to bring in the  500,000 petitions and I really thank you  for that it was really remarkable it's  phenomenal and we're beating the drum  now because of you on Capitol Hill it's  not just legislative efforts though we  must also pressure this administration  to work with our international partners  to lead the world in reducing the role  and number of nuclear weapons globally  and so I'm pleased to be calling in the  letter with to trump which Erica  mentioned reaffirming these principles  to ensure that the United States leads  efforts to prevent the spread of nuclear  weapons and materials and to pursue a  more cost-effective approach to  sustaining and upgrading the US nuclear  arsenal this administration is not just  leading us down a pathway to nuclear -  the build-up of a nuclear arms race in  fact the president intends to  aggressively militarized u.s. foreign  policy  altogether look at his budget you saw  this it's outrageous defense increase  more than 52 billion I'm on the  Appropriations Committee will tell you  who  talk to to vote against this perhaps 52  billion above last year's level  his budget puts forth a 1.4 billion  increase for the National Nuclear  Security Administration that's the NNSA  to build more bombs yet it doesn't make  our planet any safer nor does it advance  in an essays goal of nuclear  non-proliferation his ill-advised budget  priorities come at the expense of  funding other vital programs such as  housing meals on wheels for low-income  seniors and education again dr. King in  1967 said in his Riverside spree speech  he called it like it was and like it is  this is a none American agenda and we  cannot let it persist just know there  are many of us fighting tooth and nail  to eliminate the use of nuclear weapons  once and for all and believe you me I'm  not alone the Congressional Progressive  Caucus is leading the way in Congress  you've got to look at our budget I  co-chaired the global peace and security  task force excuse me and it really does  lay out not only a roadmap for  resistance but it reigns in the ongoing  waste fraud and abuse at the Pentagon  and makes smart investments in our  modern defense strategy so our military  can respond to current threats and  nuclear non-proliferation and it  decommissions our nation's cold war-era  nuclear weapons infrastructure as  outlined by the smarter approach to  nuclear expenditures and so you know you  have many members of Congress on the  front lines working for this now I have  to just mention this I work many of you  know for great statesmen congressman Ron  Dellums  I was a member of saying freeze saying  freeze some of you remember saying  freeze right now we worked on nuclear  non-proliferation and disarmament I  marched in the streets wearing my button  and carrying a sign that said a nuclear  bomb is an equal opportunity destroyer  some of you may remember that I visited  the children at Chernobyl  during a visit to Cuba after the 1986  horrific nuclear power plant accident in  the former Soviet Union I've also had  the honor of visiting Hiroshima I went  to the memorial and met relatives and  descendants of the August 6 1945 United  States and the world's first-ever use of  an atomic bomb I saw firsthand the  generations of families who carried the  kernel consequences of a nuclear attack  that experience and others reaffirm my  belief that we could not rest until we  rid the world of nuclear weapons these  personal experiences have really  informed and inspired my life to fight  for nuclear disarmament wherever I am  and whatever I am doing but you know  what we all have to live up to dr.  King's challenge to fight these three  evils as Rep Connally mentioned the  evils of racism poverty and militarism I  tell you I'm hopeful for a future where  we see our wasted war dollars reinvested  in our struggle for housing and our  struggling communities for justice an  America where we feed our hungry  children rather than building nuclear  bombs in America where no school is  without books because the memory was  spent on the money excuse me was spent  on another missile program an America  that lives up to the highest ideals of  our founding documents befitting of the  true passions of great leaders like dr.  Martin Luther King Junior's so I hope  that you will take from this conference  motivation the motivation and the  information for this resistance but this  conference is also a time to retool for  the fight ahead it's a time to pause for  planning on the path toward ridding the  world of nuclear weapons and as we learn  our lessons from the past as a movement  for nuclear disarmament  we must include people of color and the  nuclear disarmament movement which has  historically been a white progressive  movement must fight for justice and  equality because black lives do matter  black lives do matter so let's not make  those historical mistakes in our  movement as I close I am reminded of dr.  King's charge to us that peace is not  just the absence of tension but it is  the presence of justice and so let's  move on and make this a peace and  justice movement thank you again  [Applause]  [Music]
 

